;; Hodge classes on the Fermat quartic fourfold, with the algebraic cycles
;; exhibited: the space of rational Hodge classes in H^4 is computed twice
;; (Griffiths' Jacobian ring and Shioda's character decomposition), the
;; 960 planes on the fourfold are enumerated, their intersection matrix is
;; computed exactly from a combinatorial description, and its rank is shown
;; to equal the dimension of the space of Hodge classes. Every Hodge class
;; on X_4^4 is therefore a rational combination of planes.
;;
;; Sources: P. Griffiths, "On the periods of certain rational integrals",
;; Ann. Math. 90 (1969) (the Hodge filtration of a smooth hypersurface from
;; its Jacobian ring: H^{n-q,q}_prim = R_{(q+1)d - n - 2}); T. Shioda, "The
;; Hodge conjecture for Fermat varieties", Math. Ann. 245 (1979) (the
;; character decomposition of H^n_prim(X_m^n) under mu_m^{n+2}/mu_m, the
;; Hodge type of V(a) from <a> = sum <a_i/m>, and the inductive structure
;; producing algebraic cycles from lower Fermat varieties; Theorem I: if
;; every Hodge character has a pair a_i + a_j = 0 mod m, the Hodge classes
;; are algebraic, which covers m = 4 in every dimension); N. Aoki, T.
;; Shioda, "Generators of the Neron-Severi group of a Fermat surface"
;; (1983); W. Fulton, "Intersection Theory", 6.3 (excess intersection);
;; A. Weil (1949) and Katz, Deligne (SGA 4 1/2) (Fermat varieties and
;; Jacobi sums, for the character formalism).
;;
;; X = X_4^4 = {x_0^4 + ... + x_5^4 = 0} in P^5. Its middle cohomology
;; splits under G = mu_4^6 / mu_4 into lines V(a), a = (a_0..a_5) with
;; a_i in {1,2,3} and sum a_i = 0 mod 4, and V(a) lies in H^{p,q} with
;; q = <a> - 1, <a> = sum a_i / 4. A rational Hodge class is a G-orbit
;; sum over the Galois orbit {t a : t in (Z/4)^*} of type (2,2) throughout;
;; here every (2,2) character has a (2,2) Galois orbit, so the Hodge
;; classes are h^2 together with all of H^{2,2}_prim: 1 + 141 = 142, and
;; 141 is also the number of monomials of degree 6 in six variables with
;; exponents at most 2, Griffiths' count. The planes on X are the graphs
;; x_i = zeta x_j, x_k = zeta' x_l, x_m = zeta'' x_n over the 15 perfect
;; matchings of the six coordinates with zeta^4 = -1: 15 * 4^3 = 960. Two
;; such planes meet in the projectivized solution set of the union of their
;; equations, which is read off the union of the two matchings (a disjoint
;; union of even cycles and doubled edges, each consistent or not according
;; to the product of the roots of unity around it), and the intersection
;; numbers follow from excess intersection: 0 (disjoint), 1 (a point), -2
;; (a common line: c_1 of the excess line bundle is 2 - 3 - 3 + 2) and 7
;; (the self-intersection, c_2 of (1+h)^3/(1+4h)). The Gram matrix is
;; 960 x 960 with integer entries; its rank over Q is bounded below by its
;; rank modulo a prime and above by 142, so rank mod p = 142 proves rank
;; 142. Since the intersection form is positive definite on primitive
;; (2,2) classes (Hodge-Riemann), rank 142 means the planes span the whole
;; space of Hodge classes.
;;
;;   1. Griffiths: h^{3,1} = 21, h^{2,2}_prim = 141, h^{1,3} = 21 from the
;;      Jacobian ring; Shioda: 21, 141, 21 characters of those types (and
;;      183 in all); both routes agree and give b_4 = 184.
;;   2. Every (2,2) character has its Galois conjugate of type (2,2), so
;;      dim of Hodge classes = 142; every Hodge character contains a pair
;;      a_i + a_j = 0 mod 4 (Shioda's Theorem I applies: they are
;;      algebraic by the inductive structure).
;;   3. The 960 planes: each lies on X, the Gram matrix is symmetric with
;;      diagonal 7 and off-diagonal entries in {0, 1, -2}, every plane
;;      meets 33 others in a line and 299 in a point, and rank mod
;;      10007 = 142:
;;      the planes span the Hodge classes: every rational Hodge class on
;;      the Fermat quartic fourfold is algebraic, with the cycles exhibited.
;;   4. The same machinery on the Fermat quartic surface: 48 lines with
;;      L^2 = -2, Picard number 20 by the character count, Gram rank 20
;;      (the lines generate NS over Q, Lefschetz (1,1) as a computation);
;;      the Fermat quintic surface (75 lines, rho = 37) is generated by
;;      its lines too, while on the sextic (108 lines, rho = 86) the lines
;;      span only rank 62 and the Aoki-Shioda cycles are needed.
;;
;; What this does and does not prove: that every rational Hodge class on
;; this one variety is algebraic, by exhibiting cycles, independently of
;; Shioda's inductive argument (only the excess-intersection numbers and
;; Hodge-Riemann are taken from theory). Nothing beyond this variety.
;;
;; DEFECT NOTES (routed around): as in the sibling examples.

(require stdlib)

(define pass-count 0)
(define fail-count 0)
(define (check label ok)
  (if ok
      (begin (set! pass-count (+ pass-count 1)) (display "PASS: ") (display label) (newline))
      (begin (set! fail-count (+ fail-count 1)) (display "FAIL: ") (display label) (newline))))
(define (show label v) (display "  ") (display label) (display " = ") (display v) (newline))
(define (range n) (let loop ((i (- n 1)) (acc '())) (if (< i 0) acc (loop (- i 1) (cons i acc)))))
(define (all? pred xs) (cond ((null? xs) #t) ((pred (car xs)) (all? pred (cdr xs))) (else #f)))
(define (any? pred xs) (cond ((null? xs) #f) ((pred (car xs)) #t) (else (any? pred (cdr xs)))))
(define (count-if pred xs) (let loop ((l xs) (c 0)) (cond ((null? l) c) ((pred (car l)) (loop (cdr l) (+ c 1))) (else (loop (cdr l) c)))))
(define (sum xs) (let loop ((l xs) (s 0)) (if (null? l) s (loop (cdr l) (+ s (car l))))))
(define (gcd2 a b) (if (= b 0) (abs a) (gcd2 b (remainder a b))))

;; --- 1. Griffiths: Hodge numbers of the degree-m hypersurface in P^{n+1} from the Jacobian ring ------------------
;; R = C[x_0..x_{n+1}] / (x_i^{m-1}); dim R_k = coefficient of t^k in (1 + t + ... + t^{m-2})^{n+2}
(define (poly-power-coeffs m vars)               ;; coefficients of (1 + t + ... + t^{m-2})^vars
  (let loop ((v vars) (acc (vector 1)))
    (if (= v 0) acc
        (let* ((n (+ (vector-length acc) (- m 2))) (w (make-vector n 0)))
          (let li ((i 0)) (if (< i (vector-length acc)) (begin (let lj ((j 0)) (if (<= j (- m 2)) (begin (vector-set! w (+ i j) (+ (vector-ref w (+ i j)) (vector-ref acc i))) (lj (+ j 1))))) (li (+ i 1)))))
          (loop (- v 1) w)))))
(define (griffiths-hodge m n)                    ;; list of h^{n-q,q}_prim for q = 0..n
  (let ((cs (poly-power-coeffs m (+ n 2))))
    (map (lambda (q) (let ((k (- (* (+ q 1) m) (+ n 2)))) (if (or (< k 0) (>= k (vector-length cs))) 0 (vector-ref cs k)))) (range (+ n 1)))))

;; --- 2. Shioda: characters of mu_m^{n+2}/mu_m and their Hodge types ---------------------------------------------------
(define (tuples m len)                           ;; all tuples over {1..m-1}
  (if (= len 0) (list '())
      (let ((rest (tuples m (- len 1))))
        (let loop ((v (- m 1)) (acc '())) (if (= v 0) acc (loop (- v 1) (append (map (lambda (t) (cons v t)) rest) acc)))))))
(define (characters m n) (filter (lambda (a) (= 0 (modulo (sum a) m))) (tuples m (+ n 2))))
(define (bracket m a) (/ (sum a) m))             ;; <a> = sum a_i / m, an integer for a character
(define (hodge-q m a) (- (bracket m a) 1))
(define (units m) (filter (lambda (t) (= 1 (gcd2 t m))) (range m)))
(define (galois m t a) (map (lambda (x) (modulo (* t x) m)) a))
(define (hodge-character? m n a) (all? (lambda (t) (= (hodge-q m (galois m t a)) (/ n 2))) (units m)))
(define (zero-sum-pair? m a) (let loop ((l a)) (cond ((null? l) #f) ((any? (lambda (b) (= 0 (modulo (+ (car l) b) m))) (cdr l)) #t) (else (loop (cdr l))))))
(define (type-counts m n) (let ((cs (characters m n))) (map (lambda (q) (count-if (lambda (a) (= q (hodge-q m a))) cs)) (range (+ n 1)))))

(define g44 (griffiths-hodge 4 4))
(define s44 (type-counts 4 4))
(show "Fermat quartic fourfold: primitive Hodge numbers h^{4-q,q}, q = 0..4, by Griffiths and by Shioda" (list g44 s44))
(check "h^{3,1} = 21, h^{2,2}_prim = 141, h^{1,3} = 21 by both routes; 183 characters, b_4 = 184"
       (and (equal? g44 (list 0 21 141 21 0)) (equal? s44 g44) (= 183 (length (characters 4 4)))))
(define hodge-chars-44 (filter (lambda (a) (hodge-character? 4 4 a)) (characters 4 4)))
(show "number of Hodge characters (Galois orbit of type (2,2)) on X_4^4" (length hodge-chars-44))
(check "every (2,2) character is a Hodge character: the space of rational Hodge classes has dimension 1 + 141 = 142"
       (= 141 (length hodge-chars-44)))
(check "Shioda's Theorem I applies: every Hodge character of X_4^4 contains a pair a_i + a_j = 0 mod 4"
       (all? (lambda (a) (zero-sum-pair? 4 a)) hodge-chars-44))
(define hodge-dim-44 (+ 1 (length hodge-chars-44)))

;; --- 3. the planes and their intersection matrix -------------------------------------------------------------------
;; a perfect matching of {0..k-1} as a list of pairs; a cycle is (matching, exponents) with x_i = zeta_8^e x_j per pair,
;; e odd (zeta^4 = -1)
(define (matchings items)
  (if (null? items) (list '())
      (let ((a (car items)))
        (let loop ((rest (cdr items)) (seen '()) (acc '()))
          (if (null? rest) acc
              (let* ((b (car rest)) (others (append (reverse seen) (cdr rest))))
                (loop (cdr rest) (cons b seen) (append (map (lambda (mm) (cons (list a b) mm)) (matchings others)) acc))))))))
(define (exponent-tuples es len) (if (= len 0) (list '()) (let ((rest (exponent-tuples es (- len 1)))) (let loop ((l es) (acc '())) (if (null? l) acc (loop (cdr l) (append (map (lambda (t) (cons (car l) t)) rest) acc)))))))
(define (linear-cycles k order)                  ;; order = 2m: roots with zeta^m = -1 are zeta_{2m}^e, e odd
  (let ((es (filter odd? (range order))))
    (let loop ((ms (matchings (range k))) (acc '()))
      (if (null? ms) acc
          (loop (cdr ms) (append (map (lambda (e) (vector (car ms) e)) (exponent-tuples es (length (car ms)))) acc))))))
;; dimension of the affine solution space of the union of two cycles' equations: components of the union graph
(define (solution-dim k order c1 c2)
  (let ((adj (make-vector k '())))               ;; adjacency: vertex -> list of (neighbour . exponent of x_self / x_neighbour)
    (let add ((ps (vector-ref c1 0)) (es (vector-ref c1 1)))
      (if (not (null? ps))
          (let ((i (car (car ps))) (j (cadr (car ps))) (e (car es)))   ;; x_i = zeta^e x_j
            (vector-set! adj i (cons (cons j e) (vector-ref adj i)))
            (vector-set! adj j (cons (cons i (- 0 e)) (vector-ref adj j)))
            (add (cdr ps) (cdr es)))))
    (let add ((ps (vector-ref c2 0)) (es (vector-ref c2 1)))
      (if (not (null? ps))
          (let ((i (car (car ps))) (j (cadr (car ps))) (e (car es)))
            (vector-set! adj i (cons (cons j e) (vector-ref adj i)))
            (vector-set! adj j (cons (cons i (- 0 e)) (vector-ref adj j)))
            (add (cdr ps) (cdr es)))))
    ;; BFS per component assigning a potential (exponent of x_v / x_root); a conflicting edge kills the component
    (let ((pot (make-vector k #f)))
      (let loop ((v 0) (dims 0))
        (if (= v k) dims
            (if (vector-ref pot v) (loop (+ v 1) dims)
                (begin
                  (vector-set! pot v 0)
                  (let ((ok (let bfs ((frontier (list v)) (ok #t))
                              (if (null? frontier) ok
                                  (let ((u (car frontier)))
                                    (let edges ((es (vector-ref adj u)) (next (cdr frontier)) (ok ok))
                                      (if (null? es) (bfs next ok)
                                          (let ((w (car (car es))) (e (cdr (car es))))   ;; x_u = zeta^e x_w: pot(u) = pot(w) + e
                                            (if (vector-ref pot w)
                                                (edges (cdr es) next (and ok (= 0 (modulo (- (vector-ref pot u) (+ (vector-ref pot w) e)) order))))
                                                (begin (vector-set! pot w (modulo (- (vector-ref pot u) e) order)) (edges (cdr es) (cons w next) ok)))))))))))
                    (loop (+ v 1) (if ok (+ dims 1) dims))))))))))
(define (intersection-number self k order c1 c2 numbers)   ;; numbers: vector indexed by affine dimension 0.. of solution set
  (vector-ref numbers (solution-dim k order c1 c2)))
;; Gram matrix as a vector of rows
(define (gram-matrix cycles k order numbers)
  (let* ((cs (list->vector cycles)) (n (vector-length cs)) (G (make-vector n 0)))
    (let loop ((i 0))
      (if (< i n)
          (let ((row (make-vector n 0)))
            (let inner ((j 0)) (if (< j n) (begin (vector-set! row j (vector-ref numbers (solution-dim k order (vector-ref cs i) (vector-ref cs j)))) (inner (+ j 1)))))
            (vector-set! G i row) (loop (+ i 1)))))
    G))
;; rank modulo a prime, in place on a copy
(define (mod-inverse a p) (let loop ((x 1)) (if (= 1 (modulo (* a x) p)) x (loop (+ x 1)))))
(define (rank-mod-p G p)
  (let* ((m (vector-length G)) (n (vector-length (vector-ref G 0)))
         (R (list->vector (map (lambda (row) (list->vector (map (lambda (x) (modulo x p)) (vector->list row)))) (vector->list G)))))
    (let loop ((r 0) (c 0))
      (if (or (= r m) (= c n)) r
          (let ((piv (let find ((i r)) (cond ((= i m) #f) ((not (= 0 (vector-ref (vector-ref R i) c))) i) (else (find (+ i 1)))))))
            (if (not piv) (loop r (+ c 1))
                (begin
                  (let ((t (vector-ref R r))) (vector-set! R r (vector-ref R piv)) (vector-set! R piv t))
                  (let* ((prow (vector-ref R r)) (inv (mod-inverse (vector-ref prow c) p)))
                    (let rows ((i (+ r 1)))
                      (if (< i m)
                          (let ((row (vector-ref R i)))
                            (if (not (= 0 (vector-ref row c)))
                                (let ((f (modulo (* (vector-ref row c) inv) p)))
                                  (let sub ((j c)) (if (< j n) (begin (vector-set! row j (modulo (- (vector-ref row j) (* f (vector-ref prow j))) p)) (sub (+ j 1)))))))
                            (rows (+ i 1))))))
                  (loop (+ r 1) (+ c 1)))))))))

(define planes (linear-cycles 6 8))
(check "there are 15 perfect matchings of six coordinates and 960 planes on the Fermat quartic fourfold" (and (= 15 (length (matchings (range 6)))) (= 960 (length planes))))
;; intersection numbers by affine solution dimension: 0 -> empty 0, 1 -> point 1, 2 -> line -2, 3 -> the plane itself 7
(define plane-numbers (vector 0 1 -2 7))
(define G44 (with-region (gram-matrix planes 6 8 plane-numbers)))
(define (row-count-of G i v) (count-if (lambda (x) (= x v)) (vector->list (vector-ref G i))))
(show "plane 0: how many planes meet it in a line, in a point, not at all" (list (row-count-of G44 0 -2) (row-count-of G44 0 1) (row-count-of G44 0 0)))
(check "the Gram matrix is symmetric with diagonal 7 and off-diagonal entries in {0, 1, -2}"
       (and (all? (lambda (i) (= 7 (vector-ref (vector-ref G44 i) i))) (range 960))
            (all? (lambda (i) (all? (lambda (j) (and (= (vector-ref (vector-ref G44 i) j) (vector-ref (vector-ref G44 j) i)) (or (= i j) (memv (vector-ref (vector-ref G44 i) j) (list 0 1 -2))))) (range 960))) (range 960))))
(check "the row profile is uniform (the symmetry group acts transitively on the planes): every plane meets the same numbers of others in a line, a point, or not at all"
       (let ((p0 (list (row-count-of G44 0 -2) (row-count-of G44 0 1) (row-count-of G44 0 0))))
         (all? (lambda (i) (equal? p0 (list (row-count-of G44 i -2) (row-count-of G44 i 1) (row-count-of G44 i 0)))) (range 960))))
(define rank44 (with-region (rank-mod-p G44 10007)))
(show "rank of the 960 x 960 Gram matrix modulo 10007" rank44)
(check "rank mod 10007 = 142 = dim of the Hodge classes: since rank over Q is at least the rank mod p and at most 142, the 960 planes span every rational Hodge class of the Fermat quartic fourfold. Every rational Hodge class on X_4^4 is algebraic, with the cycles exhibited."
       (= rank44 hodge-dim-44))

;; --- 4. surfaces: lines on Fermat surfaces against the Picard number ----------------------------------------------------
;; lines x_i = zeta x_j, x_k = zeta' x_l with zeta^m = -1; on the degree-m surface L^2 = -2 - (m - 4) by adjunction,
;; two lines meeting in a point contribute 1, disjoint 0
(define (surface-report m)
  (let* ((lines (linear-cycles 4 (* 2 m)))
         (numbers (vector 0 1 (- -2 (- m 4))))
         (G (with-region (gram-matrix lines 4 (* 2 m) numbers)))
         (rho (+ 1 (length (filter (lambda (a) (hodge-character? m 2 a)) (characters m 2)))))
         (r (with-region (rank-mod-p G 10007))))
    (list m (length lines) rho r)))
(define surf4 (surface-report 4))
(define surf5 (surface-report 5))
(define surf6 (surface-report 6))
(show "Fermat surfaces (degree, lines, Picard number by characters, rank of the lines' Gram matrix)" (list surf4 surf5 surf6))
(check "Fermat quartic surface: 48 lines, Picard number 20, and the lines' Gram matrix has rank 20: the lines generate NS over Q"
       (equal? surf4 (list 4 48 20 20)))
(check "Fermat quintic surface: 75 lines, rho = 37, and the lines generate NS over Q (rank 37); Fermat sextic surface: 108 lines, rho = 86, but the lines span only rank 62, so the remaining 24 dimensions need the further cycles of Aoki and Shioda (Lefschetz (1,1) guarantees they exist)"
       (and (equal? surf5 (list 5 75 37 37)) (equal? surf6 (list 6 108 86 62))))
(check "Griffiths and Shioda agree on the quartic, quintic and sextic surfaces: h^{2,0}, h^{1,1}_prim, h^{0,2} = (1, 19, 1), (4, 44, 4), (10, 85, 10)"
       (and (equal? (griffiths-hodge 4 2) (type-counts 4 2)) (equal? (griffiths-hodge 5 2) (type-counts 5 2)) (equal? (griffiths-hodge 6 2) (type-counts 6 2))
            (equal? (griffiths-hodge 4 2) (list 1 19 1)) (equal? (griffiths-hodge 5 2) (list 4 44 4)) (equal? (griffiths-hodge 6 2) (list 10 85 10))))

(display "Hodge classes on the Fermat quartic fourfold: computed two ways, 960 planes, Gram rank 142, every class algebraic") (newline)
(display "Passed: ") (display pass-count) (newline)
(display "Failed: ") (display fail-count) (newline)
(if (= fail-count 0)
    (begin (display "RESULT: ALL PASS") (newline) (exit 0))
    (begin (display "RESULT: FAILURES DETECTED") (newline) (exit 1)))
